\":\"year\",\"value\":\"2019\"},\"label\":\"2019\",\"value\":19.867646},{\"period\":{\"type\":\"year\",\"value\":\"2020\"},\"label\":\"2020\",\"value\":19.119249},{\"period\":{\"type\":\"year\",\"value\":\"2021\"},\"label\":\"2021\",\"value\":37.771612},{\"period\":{\"type\":\"year\",\"value\":\"2022\"},\"label\":\"2022\",\"value\":18.076696},{\"period\":{\"type\":\"year\",\"value\":\"2023\"},\"label\":\"2023\",\"value\":17.626084}],\"drivers\":[\"Latest normal-law ACTC total-claims level is 17.626084 million in TY2023\",\"TY2021 full-refundability outlier shows a high-participation ceiling of 37.771612 million\",\"A $1 earned-income threshold should add very-low-earnings working families but not all zero-earnings families\",\"Filing incentives and awareness determine whether small refundable amounts are claimed\"],\"sourceContext\":[\"https://www.irs.gov/statistics/soi-tax-stats-individual-income-tax-returns-complete-report-publication-1304\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/18in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/19in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/20in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/21in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/22in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/23in33ar.xls\",\"https://www.irs.gov/pub/irs-soi/24in33ar.xls\"],\"runAt\":\"2026-09-23T08:21:06Z\",\"thresholdLadder\":{\"thresholds\":[14,16,17,18,19,20,21,22,23,24,26,28,31,34],\"cumulativeProbabilities\":[0.03,0.08,0.13,0.19,0.26,0.34,0.42,0.49,0.56,0.63,0.73,0.81,0.88,0.94]},\"reasoning\":[{\"kind\":\"heading\",\"text\":\"ACTC total claims, TY2027 threshold $1\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Framing and exact resolver: this conditional cell resolves only if legislation enacted by 2027-12-31 makes the IRC 24(d)(1)(B)(i) earned-income threshold no more than $1 for tax year 2027. The resolving variant is IRS SOI Publication 1304 Table 3.3, all returns total row, number of returns under refundable child tax credit or additional child tax credit, first print, annual NSA, expressed in millions. The registered resolve-by-bound is 2029-12-31; the official announcement page was fetched with the required exact URL and authenticates methodology identity, not a scheduled release day.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"fetch_source call-0003/call-0002/call-0005, then extract_irs_soi call-0012/call-0010/call-0011 for IRS Pub 1304 Table 3.3 TY2018-TY2020\",\"result\":\"Fetched official workbook values: TY2018 rawValue 20450468 and value 20.450468 million; TY2019 rawValue 19867646 and value 19.867646 million; TY2020 rawValue 19119249 and value 19.119249 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.lookup\",\"call\":\"fetch_source call-0004/call-0006/call-0001, then extract_irs_soi call-0009/call-0008/call-0007 for IRS Pub 1304 Table 3.3 TY2021-TY2023\",\"result\":\"Fetched official workbook values: TY2021 rawValue 37771612 and value 37.771612 million; TY2022 rawValue 18076696 and value 18.076696 million; TY2023 rawValue 17626084 and value 17.626084 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Reference class and base rate: the current official series, with no captured revised-methodology replacement print, gives a six-print base rate of 2018-2023 values: 20.450468, 19.867646, 19.119249, 37.771612, 18.076696, and 17.626084 million. The 2024 workbook URL returned 404 in captured call-0013, so I kept the latest six extractable official prints, TY2018-TY2023.\"},{\"kind\":\"tool\",\"tool\":\"official.calculate\",\"call\":\"calculate call-0014/call-0015/call-0020/call-0022 over the six extracted IRS values and ladder outputs\",\"result\":\"Fetched-history calculations: mean 22.151959166666664 million; sigma from stdev(values) 7.724964211235581; 1.28*sigma 9.887954190381544; ladder half-width from 16.4 to 32.0 is 7.800000000000001 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Prior/update/interval: persistence prior is the official six-print IRS history centered near the current-law/post-ARPA level, with the 2021 fully refundable value retained as an observed regime outlier; historical sample is TY2018-TY2023, values 20.450468, 19.867646, 19.119249, 37.771612, 18.076696, 17.626084 million. Adjustment components: start from a roughly 18-20 million normal-law level, add several million claims for very-low-earnings families newly eligible when the threshold is $1, keep the upper tail below 2021 because a $1 earned-income threshold is not the same as full refundability for zero-earnings families. For this annual flow/count series, sigma = 7.724964 from the fetched values themselves, so 1.28*sigma = 9.887954. The ladder-implied 80% interval is 16.4 to 32.0, half-width 7.8, about 0.79x the 1.28*sigma half-width because the 2021 full-refundability spike is only a partial analog to the narrower threshold-only condition. Final implied bounds are 16.4 and 32.0 million.\"},{\"kind\":\"math\",\"text\":\"Ladder: P(X <= 14) = 0.03; P(X <= 16) = 0.08; P(X <= 17) = 0.13; P(X <= 18) = 0.19; P(X <= 19) = 0.26; P(X <= 20) = 0.34; P(X <= 21) = 0.42; P(X <= 22) = 0.49; P(X <= 23) = 0.56; P(X <= 24) = 0.63; P(X <= 26) = 0.73; P(X <= 28) = 0.81; P(X <= 31) = 0.88; P(X <= 34) = 0.94. Linear interpolation gives P10 = 16.4, P50 = 22.142857142857142, and P90 = 32.0 million; rounded forecast fields use 16.4, 22.142857, and 32.0.\"},{\"kind\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Counter-considerations: upside risk is a broader refundable-CTC design riding with the threshold change, aggressive outreach, or simplified claiming that pulls the result toward the 2021 37.771612 million outlier and would land above the interval. Downside risk is low take-up for tiny phase-in amounts, weak low-income labor-force attachment, or a restrictive final statute, which would keep claims near the TY2023 value of 17.626084 million or below. Outside the interval below 16.4 million would require the threshold change to produce little net take-up or be offset by eligibility tightening.\"},{\"kind\":\"forecast\",\"point\":22.142857,\"ciLow\":16.4,\"ciHigh\":32}]}"}}
